<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiping Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Taiping · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taiping Rebellion was a massive civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between 1850 and 1864, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, who proclaimed a Heavenly Kingdom of Great Peace inspired by his own version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. One of the deadliest conflicts in history, it caused tens of millions of deaths and devastated central and southern China. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> treat it as a turning point that weakened dynastic authority and shifted military power to regional armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion.jpg" alt="Taiping Rebellion" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wu Youru · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regaining_the_Provincial_City_Anqing2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> suffered famines, natural disasters, economic troubles and defeats by foreign powers. The Qing military had recently lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, and large-scale illicit imports of opium upset the economy. Farmers were heavily overtaxed and rents rose, and many peasants deserted their lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s population nearly doubled between 1766 and 1833 while the cultivated land stayed the same. Banditry spread, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> and self-defence units formed. The government, commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, was increasingly corrupt and weak in the south, where local clans dominated, and anti-Manchu feeling was strongest among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> subgroup, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a> belonged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The rebellion began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> when local officials persecuted the God Worshipping Society. In early January 1851 a rebel army of about 10,000, organised by Feng Yunshan and Wei Changhui, routed Qing forces at Jintian. On 11 January 1851 Hong declared himself Heavenly King of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom. The Taiping marched north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> in December 1852 and moved down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, capturing Anqing in February 1853 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 19 March 1853. Hong renamed it Tianjing. The Taiping killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> men there and burned Manchu women to death. Their Northern expedition failed, and the Western expedition had limited success.</p><p>Hong withdrew from active administration in 1853. His clash with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xiuqing">Yang Xiuqing</a> led to the Tianjing Incident of 1856, in which Yang and his followers were slaughtered on Hong's orders, and the bloodshed split the leadership. Provincial armies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>'s Xiang Army then did what the dynasty's own troops could not. The Taiping failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, which a Qing army with European officers, the Ever Victorious Army, helped defend. Zeng's forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> from May 1862. Hong died during the siege on 1 June 1864, and the city fell barely a month later.</p><p>The war was fought without mercy on both sides. Taiping soldiers massacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, and the Qing government massacred the civilians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Both sides destroyed farmland and slaughtered the people of captured cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Taiping armies continued the fight after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> fell, and the last of them was wiped out in August 1871. Estimates of the dead range from 20 to 30 million, mostly from plague and famine, to as many as 100 million, since no reliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> existed. Thirty million people fled the conquered regions. The Qing court had to rely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>-led militias, power became partly decentralised, and Han officials held more high posts. Franz H. Michael argued that these militias evolved into the private armies of the warlords who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the Qing, though Diana Lary cited studies sceptical of this. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nian-rebellion">Nian Rebellion</a> and Muslim rebellions in the southwest and northwest continued to trouble the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taiping Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Taiping Rebellion</b> of 1850–1864 was among the most destructive civil wars in human history, with an estimated twenty to thirty million deaths.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Taiping Rebellion</b>, led by Hong Xiuquan, had held Nanjing since 1853 and much of the rich Yangzi valley; the Nian Rebellion raged north of the Huai River, and Muslim revolts broke out in Yunnan and, from 1862, in the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In West Africa Usman dan Fodio founded the Sokoto Caliphate (1804–1903), and in China Hong Xiuquan’s <b>Taiping</b> Heavenly Kingdom (1851–1864) fought the Qing in one of the most destructive wars in history.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Failure could also turn to revolt: Hong Xiuquan, after his fourth failure in the shengyuan examination, went on in the 19th century to lead the <b>Taiping Rebellion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>In Tianjing, capital of the <b>Taiping</b> Heavenly Kingdom, a conflict among its leaders killed the East King Yang Xiuqing, the North King Wei Changhui and more than 27,000 others.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing dynasty's European-trained Ever Victorious Army defeated <b>Taiping</b> forces at Cixi, but its commander, the American mercenary Frederick Townsend Ward, was mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
